{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target argues that a large centralized service is more resilient because it can coordinate global load-balancing and 'outrun' correlated failures via fast telemetry and traffic-shifting. But the problem explicitly asks the respondent to identify what evidence would distinguish failure modes that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_001 never addresses this evidentiary question at all. Worse, its own counterconditions illustrate the gap: a global load balancer logic error and shallow (identical-software) redundancy are precisely shared-cause failures that would look independent across nodes until discovered, yet the target offers no evidence standard or observational test for telling those apart from genuinely independent failures — it just lists them as 'counterconditions' and stops. The mechanism (traffic-shifting) is also self-defeating under the 'shallow redundancy' countercondition: if every node runs the same software, shifting traffic from failing nodes to identical nodes propagates or re-triggers the same fault, so the claimed resilience mechanism does not actually hold against the very correlated-cause scenario the problem highlights.","counterexample":["A large service runs 1000 identical replicated nodes behind a global load balancer. A rare input triggers a memory leak in the shared software build. Telemetry shows nodes failing 'independently' at different times as each happens to receive the bad input; the balancer shifts traffic away, but every destination runs the same vulnerable code and eventually fails too. To an observer before root-cause analysis, the failures appear uncorrelated in time and node identity; only after identifying the shared software bug does the correlation become visible. No amount of faster load-balancing 'outruns' this, and SRC_001 specifies no evidence that would distinguish this from true independence."]}]}